---
import CalculatorLayout from '../../layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import LonnsKalkulator from '../../components/calculators/LonnsKalkulator';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How is income tax calculated in Norway in 2025?',
    answer: 'Norwegian income tax for employees consists of three main components: <strong>Bracket tax (trinnskatt):</strong> a progressive tax from 1.7% to 17.6%, <strong>Social security (trygdeavgift):</strong> 7.9% on personal income above NOK 69,650, and <strong>Common tax (fellesskatt):</strong> 22% on ordinary income after deductions. You receive a standard deduction (minstefradrag) of 46% of income (max NOK 114,950) and a personal allowance (personfradrag) of NOK 70,000.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the average salary in Norway?',
    answer: 'The average monthly salary in Norway is approximately NOK 54,000 (2024 data from Statistics Norway/SSB), which equals about NOK 648,000 per year. However, salaries vary significantly by industry. Oil and gas, finance, and IT typically pay the most. The median salary is lower at around NOK 50,000/month.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is trinnskatt (bracket tax)?',
    answer: 'Trinnskatt is Norway\'s progressive income tax calculated directly on gross personal income without deductions. In 2025, it has five steps: Step 1 (1.7%) on NOK 217,400–306,050, Step 2 (4.0%) on NOK 306,050–697,150, Step 3 (13.6%) on NOK 697,150–942,400, Step 4 (16.6%) on NOK 942,400–1,410,750, and Step 5 (17.6%) above NOK 1,410,750.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is trygdeavgift (social security contribution)?',
    answer: 'Trygdeavgift is a mandatory contribution to Norway\'s National Insurance Scheme (NAV). For employees, the rate is 7.9% of gross personal income in 2025. Income below NOK 69,650 is exempt. It funds sickness benefits, parental leave, unemployment benefits, old-age pension, and disability benefits.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How accurate is this Norwegian salary calculator?',
    answer: 'This calculator uses official 2025 tax rates from Skatteetaten (the Norwegian Tax Administration) and provides reliable estimates for standard employment income. It covers trinnskatt, trygdeavgift, fellesskatt, minstefradrag, and personfradrag. Individual circumstances like union dues, student savings (BSU), mortgage interest, or special tax arrangements may affect your actual tax.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  slug="/en"
  lang="en"
  title="Norway Salary Calculator"
  metaTitle="Norway Salary Calculator 2025 | Calculate Net Pay After Tax"
  metaDescription="Free Norwegian salary calculator for 2025. Calculate net salary after trinnskatt, trygdeavgift, and common tax. Updated with official Skatteetaten rates."
  h1="Norway Salary Calculator 2025"
  intro="Calculate your net salary after tax in Norway with our free calculator. Based on official 2025 rates from Skatteetaten including bracket tax (trinnskatt), social security (trygdeavgift), and common tax (fellesskatt)."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <LonnsKalkulator client:idle />

  <section class="mt-12 prose prose-gray dark:prose-invert max-w-none" slot="content">
    <h2>Understanding Norwegian Income Tax 2025</h2>
    <p>
      Norway has a comprehensive tax system for employees that combines progressive and flat-rate components. For the 2025 tax year, your salary is subject to three main taxes: bracket tax (trinnskatt), social security contributions (trygdeavgift), and common tax (fellesskatt). This calculator helps you understand exactly how much you take home after all deductions, whether you are a Norwegian resident or an expat working in Norway.
    </p>

    <h3>Bracket Tax (Trinnskatt): Progressive Taxation</h3>
    <p>
      The bracket tax is Norway's progressive income tax, introduced in 2016 to replace the former top tax. It is calculated on gross personal income without any deductions and has five steps with rates ranging from 1.7% to 17.6%. Only income exceeding NOK 217,400 is subject to bracket tax. This progressive structure ensures that those with higher incomes contribute a larger share of their earnings in tax, reflecting Norway's commitment to income equality.
    </p>

    <h3>Social Security (Trygdeavgift): National Insurance</h3>
    <p>
      All employees in Norway pay 7.9% of their gross income (above NOK 69,650) as trygdeavgift to the National Insurance Scheme (folketrygden), administered by NAV. This contribution funds essential welfare benefits including sick pay, parental leave, unemployment benefits, old-age pension, and disability benefits. Note that employers also pay an additional employer's social security contribution (arbeidsgiveravgift) of up to 14.1% on top of the employee's salary.
    </p>

    <h3>Common Tax (Fellesskatt): Flat Rate on Ordinary Income</h3>
    <p>
      The common tax (fellesskatt) is a flat 22% tax on ordinary income (alminnelig inntekt). Unlike bracket tax, ordinary income is calculated after deductions, primarily the standard deduction (minstefradrag) of 46% of income (max NOK 114,950) and personal allowance (personfradrag) of NOK 70,000. The 22% rate is uniform across all municipalities in Norway for 2025.
    </p>
  </section>
</CalculatorLayout>
